  "Question": "If \\(\\lvert x-\\log y\\rvert=x+\\log y,\\) where \\(x\\) and \\(\\log y\\) are real, then:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x=0\\)",
    "B": "\\(y=1\\)",
    "C": "\\(x=0\\) and \\(y=1\\)",
    "D": "\\(x(y-1)=0\\)",
    "E": "None of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Let \\(L=\\log y\\). Because the logarithm is real, \\(y>0\\), and \\(L=0\\) exactly when \\(y=1\\). Splitting according to the sign of \\(x-L\\) removes the absolute-value bars.\n\nIf \\(x-L\\ge0\\), the equation becomes\n\\[\nx-L=x+L,\n\\]\nso \\(2L=0\\), giving \\(y=1\\).\n\nIf \\(x-L<0\\), the equation becomes\n\\[\nL-x=x+L,\n\\]\nso \\(2x=0\\), giving \\(x=0\\). These two cases include every real value of \\(x-L\\), including zero.\n\nNeither condition is required separately: \\((x,y)=(1,1)\\) satisfies the equation, as does \\(x=0\\) with \\(\\log y=1\\). But every allowed pair has either \\(x=0\\) or \\(y=1\\). Therefore \\(x(y-1)=0\\), choice D.",
